# Use the SESSION POOLER, not the Direct Connection string in the Supabase
# dashboard. The direct host is IPv6-only (no A record), so Render cannot
# reach it however correct the password is. Shape:
#   postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
# Port 6543 (transaction mode) also works but needs pgx's prepared-statement
# cache disabled; 5432 is what pgxpool's defaults expect.
DATABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# KIE_API_KEY authenticates requests to kie.ai, the only video vendor this
# worker speaks to (fal.ai is decommissioned). Required.
KIE_API_KEY=
# KIE_WEBHOOK_SECRET verifies inbound kie.ai webhook signatures
# (internal/http/webhook_kie.go). Optional — kie's webhook signing is
# opt-in on THEIR side (a webhookHmacKey generated in kie's own settings),
# so leaving this unset is a legitimate, if degraded, state: /webhooks/kie
# rejects every callback and a finished render is noticed only by the
# reconcile poll, 45-90s late (config.go logs a startup Warning when this
# is empty, naming exactly that cost).
KIE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
APP_BASE_URL=
RENDER_CONCURRENCY=10
FFMPEG_CONCURRENCY=2
PORT=8080
# ASSEMBLE_ENABLED=1 turns on ffmpeg-concatenating each project's clips into
# a delivered final.mp4. Off by default — whether the client wants an
# assembled video or raw clips is still open (see docs/roadmap.md).
ASSEMBLE_ENABLED=0
# debug|info|warn|error — internal/logging.levelFromEnv, defaults to info.
LOG_LEVEL=info
# MODE=development|production, defaulting to development (config.go's
# parseMode). development simulates kie submissions — a canned video
# after ~3s with a ~10% synthetic failure rate, so the retry/reconcile/
# failed paths get exercised — and never calls kie for real; production
# makes every render real and billable.
#
# Replaced the old MOCK_FAL_VIDEO, whose independence from web/
# .env.example's own MOCK_CLAUDE/MOCK_FAL_IMAGE was exactly the incident
# web/lib/mode.ts's header describes: a render mocked locally by this flag
# was claimed and billed by the DEPLOYED worker anyway, because both poll
# the same scenes table and a flag set on one process has no say in which
# claims a row first. The actual fix is scenes.dev
# (0063_scenes_dev_flag.sql): this worker's ClaimQueuedScenes/
# StaleRendering (internal/store/store.go) only ever claim or
# reconcile rows whose dev column matches THIS value, so a development
# worker set here can no longer touch a production row regardless of what
# this variable says on the OTHER service.
MODE=development
# AUDIO_ENABLED=1 lets scenes with is_talking_head=true route to a
# lip-synced audio model (kie.PickModel, but only when the project has
# explicitly selected Kling — see that function's own doc comment); with it
# off, every scene renders on its project's silent default regardless of
# is_talking_head.
#
# THE OLD REASON FOR DEFAULTING THIS OFF IS NO LONGER TRUE, and it is worth
# writing down rather than quietly deleting. This block used to say audio
# was "the expensive" route and "~60% of the bill", which is why it shipped
# as 0. That was measured when the audio model was fal's. It is now Kling
# 3.0 Pro on kie at 18 cr/s silent / 27 cr/s with sound, against Seedance
# 2.5's 28-114 cr/s depending on resolution — Kling with sound on is
# frequently CHEAPER per second than the silent default, not more
# expensive. Anyone reading the old sentence would turn this off to save
# money and spend more.
#
# Both deployed services already declare "1" (render.yaml), so 0 here was
# also a lie about what production does — the local envs were the outlier
# and every talking-head scene rendered silent locally while production
# rendered lip-synced.
#
# MUST match web/.env.example's AUDIO_ENABLED — the worker bills against
# this flag, the web app's cost estimate reads the same flag, and the two
# disagreeing means the operator sees a price the worker won't charge.
AUDIO_ENABLED=1
# CAPTIONS_ENABLED=1 is meant to turn on word-synced caption burn-in on the
# assembled final (internal/captions, internal/loop/assemble.go), but has NO
# EFFECT today: captioning required fal-ai/whisper transcription, deleted
# along with fal.ai, and there is no replacement transcription vendor — see
# internal/captions/captions.go's package doc comment. Left declared (and
# defaulted to 0, matching what has always shipped) so a future replacement
# vendor doesn't need this variable introduced retroactively; see
# config.go's own doc comment on config.Config.CaptionsEnabled.
CAPTIONS_ENABLED=0
# How long (minutes) a scene may sit in 'rendering' with reconcile
# repeatedly failing to make progress on it — ingest (download/normalize/
# upload), the kie status check, the kie result fetch, or the scene
# re-fetch — before reconcile gives up and marks it 'failed' outright, so a
# permanently-broken render doesn't retry forever invisibly. Defaults to 30.
RECONCILE_GIVE_UP_AFTER_MINUTES=30
